implementationPlan.description:
THE REFUSAL CODES AND THEIR CLOSED LAYER MAP. NEW `apps/daemon/src/repository/env-example-sync-contracts.ts` plus its `.js` bridge.

**WHY A DAEMON-SIDE MODULE ON A PUBLICATION ROW:** that is exactly what the `release.decide` publication did. `task-bda0eeac`'s `filesModified` shows `apps/daemon/src/release/release-decide-contracts{.ts,.js,.test.ts}` alongside the shared-contract and mirror files, and its DoD item 3 required the publication row to declare the codes and the one frozen map. Codes are daemon vocabulary; the KIND is shared contract. This row publishes both halves of the vocabulary so the consumer imports rather than re-declares.

MIRROR `release-decide-contracts.ts` STRUCTURE EXACTLY:
  - the kind constant, re-exported from the shared tuple so the string is written ONCE;
  - ONE frozen code-to-layer map covering every code;
  - the code list DERIVED from that map's keys — never restated, so the two cannot disagree;
  - a refusal factory taking the CODE ONLY and reading the layer from the map, accepting NO layer argument, so no call site can mint a disagreeing pair;
  - a narrowing predicate for an unknown value, in the style of `isReleaseDecideRefusal`.

**FOUR CODES, MATCHING WHAT THE CONSUMER ACTUALLY THROWS** (task-f1e402960ada4be7adc6410ec04c945a): the approved contract is absent or unapproved; the project has no bound repository; the repository is unreadable; the write or commit failed. **Publish no speculative fifth** — an unthrown code is a claim that something can refuse when nothing does.

**EVERY LAYER REUSES A ROSTERED LAYER** (risk F). A new `*_LAYER` constant is a coupled roster backfill plus `pnpm test:security`, explicitly out of scope. If a needed layer does not exist, STOP and report. Never write a bare string literal at a throw site — `tests/security/boundary-roster.security.ts` cannot enumerate one, and `daemon-command-registry.ts:206` is the existing instance of that hole.

Under 250 lines. The `.js` bridge needs the name-matched `.test.ts` sibling from step 5 or `runtime-entrypoint.test.ts` reports it as `unexpected`.
